# Line endings are LF everywhere — in the repository AND in the working tree.
#
# Without this, the working-tree form depends on each contributor's
# core.autocrlf (Git for Windows ships it enabled at the system level), so a
# Windows checkout gets CRLF files while the committed blobs stay LF. Pinning
# it here makes a checkout byte-identical on every platform, which is what a
# contributor comparing files or running a byte-level diff expects.
#
# text=auto still lets Git detect binary files and leave them untouched.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The one exception, armed before it is needed: cmd.exe reads a batch file by
# seeking through it, so an LF-only .bat/.cmd can mis-resolve `goto` labels and
# multi-line `for`/`if` blocks. The repository contains none today; the rule is
# here so that adding one later cannot walk into it. The blob stays LF either
# way — only the checkout form differs.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# There is deliberately NO speculative rule for byte-exact text fixtures (raw
# HTTP/MIME samples, signed golden files), whose CRLF is load-bearing. No glob
# predicts where such a file would land, and a glob that matches nothing reads
# as protection while providing none. When one appears, mark ITS path `-text`
# (not `eol=crlf`: only `-text` preserves the committed bytes in both
# directions). Binary files are already safe — `text=auto` detects them.
